Description: Clipboard history for Linux desktops
 A clipboard manager that keeps a searchable history of what you copy, in the
 style of the Windows 10/11 clipboard flyout. Press Ctrl+` to open a compact
 panel near the pointer, type to filter, and press Return to put an entry back
 on the clipboard.
 .
 Features:
  * Text, multi-line text, URLs, images and file selections
  * Instant fuzzy search that stays fast on a large history
  * Pinned entries that survive cleanup, restart and reboot
  * Per-entry edit, view, copy, pin and delete
  * Configurable history size and retention
  * Light, dark and system themes
 .
 It works on X11 and on Wayland, under GNOME, KDE Plasma, Xfce, Cinnamon and
 MATE. On Wayland it uses the wlr-data-control protocol where the compositor
 provides it, and falls back to reading the X11 clipboard through Xwayland on
 GNOME, which mutter keeps synchronised.
 .
 Clipboard data never leaves the machine. There is no network code, no
 telemetry and no account; the systemd unit runs with PrivateNetwork=true. The
 history is stored in the user's own directory with owner-only permissions, and
 clipboard contents are never written to the log.
